<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<worksheet xml:id="Math234Fa24-Midterm1" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
	<title>Math234Fa24-Midterm1</title>
	<exercise>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				Let <m>\vec{a} = \langle 2, -3, 1 \rangle</m> and <m>\vec{b} = \langle 1, 4, -2 \rangle.</m>
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Find the unit vector in the direction of <m>\vec{a}.</m>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>|\vec{a}| = \sqrt{2^2 + (-3)^2 + 1^2} = \sqrt{4+9+1} = \sqrt{14}</me>. Unit vector <me>\vec{u} = \frac{\vec{a}}{|\vec{a}|} = \langle 2/\sqrt{14}, -3/\sqrt{14}, 1/\sqrt{14} \rangle</me>.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Find the vector projection of <m>\vec{b}</m> onto <m>\vec{a}.</m>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>\text{proj}_{\vec{a}}\vec{b} = \frac{\vec{a} \cdot \vec{b}}{|\vec{a}|^2}\vec{a} = \frac{2(1) - 3(4) + 1(-2)}{14}\langle 2, -3, 1 \rangle = \frac{-12}{14}\langle 2, -3, 1 \rangle = -\frac{6}{7}\langle 2, -3, 1 \rangle.</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				Find an equation for the plane that contains the points <m>A = (1,0,1),</m> <m>B = (3, 2, 0)</m> and <m>C = (4, 1, 2).</m>
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<solution>
			<p>
				Vectors: <me>\vec{AB} = \langle 2, 2, -1 \rangle, \vec{AC} = \langle 3, 1, 1 \rangle.</me>
				Normal: <me>\vec{n} = \vec{AB} \times \vec{AC} = \langle 3, -5, -4 \rangle.</me>
				Plane: <me>3(x-1) - 5(y-0) - 4(z-1) = 0 \implies 3x - 5y - 4z = -1.</me>
			</p>
		</solution>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				Find an equation for the <term>line segment</term> connecting the points <m>A = (1,4, 2)</m> and <m>B = (5, 3, 8).</m>
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<solution>
			<p>
				<me>\vec{r}(t) = (1-t)\langle 1, 4, 2 \rangle + t\langle 5, 3, 8 \rangle = \langle 1+4t, 4-t, 2+6t \rangle, 0 \le t \le 1.</me>
			</p>
		</solution>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				Consider the surface <m>x^2 =4y^2 + z^2</m>.
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Sketch the traces of the form <m>x = k</m>, with <m>k =0, 1</m> in the <m>yz</m>-plane where the <m>y</m> axis is horizontal. Label your traces.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>x=0 \implies 4y^2 + z^2 = 0 \implies (0,0); x=1 \implies 4y^2 + z^2 = 1 \text{ (ellipse)}</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Sketch the traces of the form <m>y = k</m>, with <m>k =0, 1</m> in the <m>xz</m>-plane where the <m>x</m> axis is horizontal. Label your traces.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>y=0 \implies x^2 = z^2 \implies x = \pm z \text{ (lines)}; y=1 \implies x^2 = 4 + z^2 \implies x^2 - z^2 = 4 \text{ (hyperbola)}</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				If the limit exists, compute it (making sure to justify your answer). If the limit does not exist, justify why it doesn’t exist. If you use a theorem, state clearly which theorem you are using. Make sure to use correct limit notation. <me>\displaystyle\lim_{(x,y) \to (0,0)} \dfrac{xy^2}{2x^2 + y^4}</me>
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<solution>
			<p>
				Along <m>x=0</m>: <me>\lim_{y \to 0} \frac{0}{y^4} = 0.</me>
				Along <m>y^2 = x</m>: <me>\lim_{x \to 0} \frac{x(x)}{2x^2 + x^2} = \lim_{x \to 0} \frac{x^2}{3x^2} = \frac{1}{3}.</me>
				Since limits are different, the limit does not exist.
			</p>
		</solution>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				At what point(s) does the curve <m>\vec{r}(t) = \langle 4t^2+6t, 0, t \rangle</m> intersect the paraboloid <m>x = y^2+z^2</m> ? Make sure to give the coordinate(s) of the point(s) in your final answer.
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<solution>
			<p>
				Substitute curve into paraboloid: <me>4t^2 + 6t = 0^2 + t^2 \implies 3t^2 + 6t = 0 \implies 3t(t+2) = 0.</me>
				So <m>t=0</m> or <m>t=-2</m>.
				Points: <m>\vec{r}(0) = (0,0,0)</m> and <m>\vec{r}(-2) = (4, 0, -2)</m>.
			</p>
		</solution>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				Consider the curve given by <m>\vec{r}(t)=\langle t, t^2, 2t+t^3\rangle.</m>
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Find a tangent vector to the curve at the point <m>(2,4,12).</m>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>\vec{r}\,'(t) = \langle 1, 2t, 2+3t^2 \rangle \implies \vec{r}\,'(2) = \langle 1, 4, 14 \rangle</me>.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Write down an integral that represents the length of the curve, with <m>1 \le t \le 2.</m> You do NOT need to evaluate the integral.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>L = \int_1^2 \sqrt{1^2 + (2t)^2 + (2+3t^2)^2} \, dt.</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Find an equation for the tangent line to the curve at the point <m>(2,4,12).</m>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>\vec{L}(s) = \langle 2, 4, 12 \rangle + s\langle 1, 4, 14 \rangle.</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Find an equation for the normal plane to the curve at the point <m>(2,4,12).</m> That is, find an equation for the plane that is perpendicular to the tangent line to the curve at the given point.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>1(x-2) + 4(y-4) + 14(z-12) = 0 \implies x + 4y + 14z = 186.</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				Consider the surfaces <m>z = \sqrt{x^2+y^2}</m> and <m>z = 2+x.</m> Find a vector function that represents the curve of intersection of the given surfaces.
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<solution>
			<p>
				<me>\sqrt{x^2+y^2} = 2+x \implies x^2+y^2 = 4 + 4x + x^2 \implies y^2 = 4x + 4 \implies x = \frac{1}{4}y^2 - 1.</me>
				Parametrization: <me>\vec{r}(t) = \langle \frac{1}{4}t^2-1, t, 2 + \frac{1}{4}t^2-1 \rangle = \langle \frac{1}{4}t^2-1, t, \frac{1}{4}t^2+1 \rangle.</me>
			</p>
		</solution>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				Consider the curve given by <m>\vec{r}(t)=\langle 3+2\sin(t),2t,2\cos(t)\rangle</m>
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Find the unit tangent vector, <m>\vec{T}(t).</m>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>
						\begin{aligned}
						\vec{r}\,'(t) \amp = \langle 2\cos t, 2, -2\sin t \rangle \implies |\vec{r}\,'(t)| = \sqrt{4\cos^2 t + 4 + 4\sin^2 t} = \sqrt{8} = 2\sqrt{2} \\
						\vec{T}(t) \amp = \langle \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\cos t, \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}, -\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\sin t \rangle
						\end{aligned}
					</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Find the unit normal vector, <m>\vec{N}(t).</m>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>
						\begin{aligned}
						\vec{T}\,'(t) \amp = \langle -\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\sin t, 0, -\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\cos t \rangle \implies |\vec{T}\,'(t)| = \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} \\
						\vec{N}(t) \amp = \langle -\sin t, 0, -\cos t \rangle
						\end{aligned}
					</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Find the curvature, <m>\kappa(t).</m>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>\kappa(t) = \frac{|\vec{T}\,'(t)|}{|\vec{r}\,'(t)|} = \frac{1/\sqrt{2}}{2\sqrt{2}} = \frac{1}{4}.</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				Consider the function <m>f(x,y) = \dfrac{\cos(y+x)}{ e^{x^2 -y}\sqrt{y^3-x}}.</m>
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Find the domain of the function.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>y^3 - x > 0 \implies y^3 > x \implies y > \sqrt[3]{x}.</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Sketch the domain of the function. Shade the region(s) that are part of the domain. Use dotted lines when sketching a curve that is NOT in the domain, and solid lines when sketching curves that are.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					The domain is the region above the curve <m>y = x^{1/3}</m>, with the curve being dotted (not included).
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				An object’s acceleration is given by <me>\vec{a}(t) = \langle \sin(\pi t), e^{-t}, 2t +3 \rangle.</me> Its initial velocity is <m>\vec{v}(0) = \langle 1,0,2 \rangle.</m> Find a vector equation <m>\vec{v}(t)</m> for the velocity of the object at time <m>t.</m>
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<solution>
			<p>
				<me>
					\begin{aligned}
					\vec{v}(t) \amp = \vec{v}(0) + \int_0^t \vec{a}(u) \, du = \langle 1, 0, 2 \rangle + \left\langle \int_0^t \sin(\pi u) \, du, \int_0^t e^{-u} \, du, \int_0^t (2u+3) \, du \right\rangle \\
					\amp = \langle 1, 0, 2 \rangle + \langle \frac{1}{\pi}(1-\cos \pi t), 1-e^{-t}, t^2+3t \rangle \\
					\amp = \langle 1 + \frac{1}{\pi}(1-\cos \pi t), 1-e^{-t}, t^2+3t+2 \rangle
					\end{aligned}
				</me>
			</p>
		</solution>
	</exercise>
</worksheet>
